---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditOverlayEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditOverlayEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="highlight-pdf">
  <PdfEditOverlayEditor client:load mode="highlight" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>PDF highlighting that behaves like a real marker</h2>
      <p>
        A semi-transparent rectangle over text gives a washed-out grey block. It lightens the black
        letters as much as it tints the white paper, so the passage becomes harder to read.
      </p>
      <p>
        Marker ink filters light. It darkens the paper and leaves black text black. That is multiply
        blending, and this tool writes it into the PDF. Yellow over white paper gives yellow, and yellow
        over black type gives black. The passage stays crisp.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Choosing a colour</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[600px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Colour</th>
              <th scope="col">On screen</th>
              <th scope="col">Photocopied or printed mono</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Yellow</td>
              <td>Bright, unmistakable</td>
              <td>Very light grey. Text stays fully legible</td>
              <td>Everything, unless you need a second colour</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Green</td>
              <td>Calm, reads as "approved"</td>
              <td>Mid grey</td>
              <td>A second category on a screen-only document</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Blue</td>
              <td>Cool, low contrast with blue links</td>
              <td>Dark grey. Can hide small type</td>
              <td>Screen reading only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Red</td>
              <td>Alarming, hard to read over</td>
              <td>Dark grey</td>
              <td>Short flags, not passages</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Use about 40% opacity. Lower, it disappears when the page is printed. Higher, even multiply
        blending starts to compete with the type.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Highlighting line by line</h2>
      <p>
        Drag one rectangle per line of text. It takes a few seconds longer than one big block, but the
        highlight looks right: the last line of a paragraph is usually short, and a full-width block over
        it highlights a lot of empty paper.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cover from just below the baseline to just above the ascenders. A rectangle that goes into the
        line above is the most common and most visible mistake. Zoom in to 150% or 200% to align it
        easily.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your text stays searchable</h2>
      <p>
        The highlight is a coloured rectangle on the page, so the text under it does not change. Ctrl+F
        still finds it, a screen reader still reads it, <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to Text</a> still
        extracts it, and the file size barely changes, because a highlight is a few bytes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tools that flatten the page to an image to "burn in" the highlight destroy the text layer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Studying from a highlighted PDF</h2>
      <p>
        For revision, highlight sparingly. If half a page is highlighted, nothing stands out. Use at
        most one highlight per paragraph, plus a margin note in the <strong>Text</strong> tool that says
        why it matters. When you come back to the document, read the notes. The highlights show your eye
        where to look.
      </p>
      <p>
        To print the marked-up copy, check it in grayscale first with
        <a href="/grayscale-pdf">PDF to Grayscale</a>. That shows how each colour prints on a mono
        printer before you use paper.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
